  17: Median model latency per step over every step of each run, measured while the emulators and other model servers were
  18: running (llama.cpp Metal):
  19: 
  20: | Model | Quantization | Steps | Seconds per step, median (10th–90th percentile) | Prompt tokens | Output tokens |
  21: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
  22: | **v4 (ours)** | Q4_K_M | 1,706 | **2.96** (1.7–4.6) | 1,303 | 22 |
  23: | GUI-Owl-1.5-2B | Q4_K_M | 2,228 | 7.00 (4.6–10.4) | 2,106 | 47 |
  24: | MAI-UI-2B (run in progress: 17 of 116 episodes) | Q8_0 (Q4_K_M breaks its output: results/maiui_quant_check.md) | 324 | 8.46 (6.7–11.5) | 2,006 | 53 |
  25: 
  26: 
  27: ## Real phone: Snapdragon 8 Elite Extreme Gen 6 (Qualcomm Device Cloud)
  28: 
  29: _Run in progress._
  30: 
  31: Earlier attempt on a Pixel 9 (Tensor G4, Android 16) through Firebase Android Device Streaming, stopped after v4 when we switched to Qualcomm Device Cloud: v4 median 74.4 s/step over 11 calls, rising from 43.1 to 91.2 s: from the 5th call on, battery temperature had risen from 27 to ~36 °C and the big cores were capped from 3.1 GHz to ~1.8–2.0 GHz (prompt speed fell from ~26 to ~14 tok/s) while Android's thermal status still read 0 (raw data and thermal log in the working repo, `results/devstream/`).
  32: 
  33: 
  34: ## How it was measured
  35: 
  36: * **CPU benchmark.** Host: GCP n2-standard-16 (Intel Xeon @ 2.80 GHz), otherwise idle. Each call is the first step of a
  37:   fresh episode on one of the 5 screenshots with that task's real goal, driven through the evaluation agent code (LFM3
  38:   agent with the v4 prompt; MobileWorld's official GUI-Owl agent; MobileWorld's official MAI-UI agent with one
  39:   screenshot). One warm-up call, then 3 passes. Latency = wall time of `agent.predict()` (prompt building, image
  40:   encoding, HTTP, model, parsing); prompt/output timings come from the server's per-request log.
  41: * **Prompt cache.** This llama.cpp build keeps a RAM prompt cache (`--cache-ram`, 8 GB default), so GUI-Owl's and
  42:   MAI-UI's repeated screenshots in passes 2–3 were served from cache. Only calls whose prompt was actually processed
  43:   are kept (v4 14, GUI-Owl 4, MAI-UI 4; the kept calls are tightly clustered). The cache also inflated GUI-Owl/MAI-UI
  44:   server memory (~7.5 GB RSS), so memory is shown as model file size; v4's server used 2.8 GB.
  45: * **Phones.** The model runs ON the phone: llama.cpp b12110 (official Android arm64 build = `cd959bae9`), CPU,
  46:   `-t 4 -np 1 --cache-ram 0`, the evaluation flags of each model; model files downloaded on the phone and SHA-256-checked
  47:   against the files used everywhere else; the same 5 screenshots and agent code as the CPU benchmark, driven from the
  48:   Mac. Each request went through a recording proxy that wrote the phone-side llama.cpp timings to disk as it
  49:   completed. Order: pass 1 v4 → GUI-Owl → MAI-UI, pass 2 reversed; the first call of each model in each pass is a
  50:   warm-up on a mirrored screenshot and is excluded. Phone-side prompt time counts every prompt token (no prefix reuse on
  51:   the phone), unlike the CPU table where llama.cpp reused the cached system prompt. End-to-end latency includes the
  52:   SSH/adb tunnel to the device lab (~2–3 s).
  53: * **MAI-UI on the M5 runs at Q8_0.** At Q4_K_M MAI-UI-2B's output collapses into empty `<thinking>` loops on
  54:   AndroidWorld screens ([evidence](results/maiui_quant_check.md)); its AndroidWorld arm therefore uses the 8-bit file
  55:   (1.83 GB + 0.45 GB projector), which is also somewhat slower per token than Q4_K_M. Its CPU row above is Q4_K_M
  56:   (on the MobileWorld screenshots used there its output was well-formed).
  57: 
  58: ## Why v4 is faster
  59: 
  60: Prompt processing (including the vision encoder) dominates on CPU: v4 sends ~800 prompt tokens per step (~750 image
  61: tokens) against ~2,570 for GUI-Owl/MAI-UI, and processes them ~3× faster per token on this CPU (149 vs ~47 tok/s; LFM3
  62: is a hybrid convolution/attention model). v4 also writes shorter outputs (28 vs 47/90 tokens) and generates ~2× faster.
  63: The smaller image budget is part of the trade-off: GUI-Owl's higher seed-30 accuracy (23 vs 18) costs ~2.4× (M5 GPU) to
  64: ~9× (CPU) more time per step. A phone CPU/NPU will be slower in absolute terms; the ratios are the useful part.

