FILE /Users/alpha/lfm3-android-runs/templates/SPEED.md
1 # Speed: v4 vs GUI-Owl-1.5-2B vs MAI-UI-2B
2 
3 ## CPU: Intel Xeon 2.8 GHz, 8 threads, idle GCP VM
4 
5 Same 5 real 1080×2400 screenshots and goals for every model, run through each model's actual evaluation agent code,
6 4-bit models served by llama.cpp (`cd959bae9`, `llama-server -np 1 -t 8`, same flags as in the evaluations):
7 
8 {{speed_cpu}}
9 
10 ## Apple M5 GPU, from the real AndroidWorld seed-30 runs
11 
12 Median model latency per step over every step of each run, measured while the emulators and other model servers were
13 running (llama.cpp Metal):
14 
15 {{speed_m5}}
16 
17 ## Real phone: Snapdragon 8 Elite Extreme Gen 6 (Qualcomm Device Cloud)
18 
19 {{speed_qdc}}
20 
21 ## How it was measured
22 
23 * **CPU benchmark.** Host: GCP n2-standard-16 (Intel Xeon @ 2.80 GHz), otherwise idle. Each call is the first step of a
24   fresh episode on one of the 5 screenshots with that task's real goal, driven through the evaluation agent code (LFM3
25   agent with the v4 prompt; MobileWorld's official GUI-Owl agent; MobileWorld's official MAI-UI agent with one
26   screenshot). One warm-up call, then 3 passes. Latency = wall time of `agent.predict()` (prompt building, image
27   encoding, HTTP, model, parsing); prompt/output timings come from the server's per-request log.
28 * **Prompt cache.** This llama.cpp build keeps a RAM prompt cache (`--cache-ram`, 8 GB default), so GUI-Owl's and
29   MAI-UI's repeated screenshots in passes 2–3 were served from cache. Only calls whose prompt was actually processed
30   are kept (v4 14, GUI-Owl 4, MAI-UI 4; the kept calls are tightly clustered). The cache also inflated GUI-Owl/MAI-UI
31   server memory (~7.5 GB RSS), so memory is shown as model file size; v4's server used 2.8 GB.
32 * **Phones.** The model runs ON the phone: llama.cpp b12110 (official Android arm64 build = `cd959bae9`), CPU,
33   `-t 4 -np 1 --cache-ram 0`, the evaluation flags of each model; model files downloaded on the phone and SHA-256-checked
34   against the files used everywhere else; the same 5 screenshots and agent code as the CPU benchmark, driven from the
35   Mac. Each request went through a recording proxy that wrote the phone-side llama.cpp timings to disk as it
36   completed. Order: pass 1 v4 → GUI-Owl → MAI-UI, pass 2 reversed; the first call of each model in each pass is a
37   warm-up on a mirrored screenshot and is excluded. Phone-side prompt time counts every prompt token (no prefix reuse on
38   the phone), unlike the CPU table where llama.cpp reused the cached system prompt. End-to-end latency includes the
39   SSH/adb tunnel to the device lab (~2–3 s).
40 * **MAI-UI on the M5 runs at Q8_0.** At Q4_K_M MAI-UI-2B's output collapses into empty `<thinking>` loops on
41   AndroidWorld screens ([evidence](results/maiui_quant_check.md)); its AndroidWorld arm therefore uses the 8-bit file
42   (1.83 GB + 0.45 GB projector), which is also somewhat slower per token than Q4_K_M. Its CPU row above is Q4_K_M
43   (on the MobileWorld screenshots used there its output was well-formed).
44 
45 ## Why v4 is faster
46 
47 Prompt processing (including the vision encoder) dominates on CPU: v4 sends ~800 prompt tokens per step (~750 image
48 tokens) against ~2,570 for GUI-Owl/MAI-UI, and processes them ~3× faster per token on this CPU (149 vs ~47 tok/s; LFM3
49 is a hybrid convolution/attention model). v4 also writes shorter outputs (28 vs 47/90 tokens) and generates ~2× faster.
50 The smaller image budget is part of the trade-off: GUI-Owl's higher seed-30 accuracy (23 vs 18) costs ~2.4× (M5 GPU) to
51 ~9× (CPU) more time per step. A phone CPU/NPU will be slower in absolute terms; the ratios are the useful part.

FILE /Users/alpha/lfm3-android-runs/templates/RUNS.md
1 # Training runs
2 
3 All LFM3-VL-2B versions use the same recipe and budget so that data/prompt changes are the only variable: LoRA r64 /
4 α128 on the language model only (vision tower untouched), lr 2e-4, 1,250 optimizer steps × 32 rows (batch 8 × grad-accum 4),
5 pixel budget 880×880, one screenshot + text history per step, output `<think>intent</think>` + one JSON action.
6 Checkpoints were pushed to the hub every 50 steps so a lost VM could resume.
7 
8 {{table_training}}
9 
10 Columns come from each run's `run_config.json` / `train_summary.json` / `train_log.jsonl` (the `source` path in
11 `data/runs.json`). "epochs seen" = fraction of the training set covered by 1,250 × 32 rows.
12 
13 ## What each version changed and what happened
14 
15 | version | change | result (AndroidWorld seed 30) | lesson |
16 |---|---|---|---|
17 | **v1** | AndroidControl only (official train episodes, high-level goals) | 15/116 | strong action baseline; never emits `answer` |
18 | **v2** | + GUI-Odyssey, AMEX, ScreenQA (v2 mix) | 3/116 (vs v1 0–12, p = 4.9e-04) | 8,739 rows had a **blank intent** (`<think></think>`): the model learned to act without planning |
19 | v2 + forced intent | inference-only ablation: force a one-line intent | 6/116 | confirms the blank-intent diagnosis, not a fix |
20 | v1 + runtime feedback | inference-only ablation: lexical app resolution + failure notes | 10/116 | worse; dropped |
21 | **v3** | v2 mix with every intent filled | 13/116 (vs v1 4–6, p = 0.75) | offline gains on Odyssey/AMEX/ScreenQA did not transfer to AndroidWorld |
22 | v4 (data 570febb) | first v4 build | — (abandoned before evaluation) | Codex data audit returned STOP (answer-leaking goals, guard gaps); retrained on rebuilt data |
23 | **v4 (v4b data)** | v3 rewritten to the v4 prompt (answer-vs-complete rules + the device's installed-app list) + navigate-then-answer episodes built from AndroidControl *train* episodes (questions/answers from Qwen3-VL-8B, verified by a second call and a strict typed check) + shortened ScreenQA answers | **18/116** (6 answer + 12 action) | fixed "never answers"; passed the gate; frozen as LFM3 final |
24 | v4-RL | 200 steps of static-screenshot GRPO from v4 (LoRA r16, KL 0.001, 4 samples/state) | not run (failed offline promotion) | answering up (0.868 → 0.890) but AndroidControl high-level step SR down (0.728 → 0.667); 60 % of groups had zero reward variance; 60 % of the state pool were terminal screens |
25 
26 Other base models with the v1 recipe: Qwen3.5-2B (16/116), Qwen3.5-0.8B (12/116), LFM2.5-VL-450M (2/116). Qwen3.5-2B v2
27 mirrors LFM3 v2 (same mix).
28 
29 ## RL run details (v4-RL)
30 
31 {{rl_details}}
32 Promotion rule (pre-registered before training ended): strict dev_answer ≥ +0.05, dev_complete false answers ≤ 0.03,
33 AndroidControl high-level step SR ≥ −0.02, then AndroidWorld seed 30 ≥ v4. Outcome: [results/v4rl_promotion_offline.json](results/v4rl_promotion_offline.json) — fails (a) and (c).

